      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, and then multiply these two values together.\n\n### Step 1: Find the GCD of 18 and 42\nThe greatest common divisor is the largest positive integer that divides both numbers without leaving a remainder.\n\nFirst, list the factors of each number:\n- Factors of 18: \\(1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18\\)\n- Factors of 42: \\(1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 21, 42\\)\n\nThe common factors are:\n\\[ \\{1, 2, 3, 6\\} \\]\n\nThe greatest common factor among these is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Find the LCM of 18 and 42\nThe least common multiple is the smallest positive integer that is a multiple of both numbers.\n\nTo find the LCM, we can use the relationship between GCD and LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation with the given values:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) \\times 6 = 18 \\times 42\n\\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) \\times 6 = 756\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can solve for the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = \\frac{756}{6} = 126\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Multiply the GCD and the LCM\nNow we multiply the GCD and the LCM together:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{756}\n\\]",
